<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charente-Maritime</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charente-Maritime is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the Atlantic coast, in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>. It covers the historic province of Aunis and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, together with the islands of Ré and Oléron. Created in 1790 as Charente-Inférieure, it took its present name in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charente-maritime.jpg" alt="Charente-Maritime" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pertuisallionis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%B4tel_Lanusse,_pr%C3%A9fecture_(La_Rochelle).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Charente-Maritime lies on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, named after the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, with an area of 6,864 square kilometres. It was created by a decree of the Constituent Assembly of 22 December 1789, which took effect on 4 March 1790, as one of the 83 original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. It encompasses most of the former province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, nearly all of Aunis and the Pays d'Aulnay from Poitou. Initially named Charente-Inférieure after the lower course of the river, it was renamed on 4 September 1941, reflecting its Atlantic coast identity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>, the capital of Saintonge, was the prefecture from 1790 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> decreed its transfer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> in 1810.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a> skeleton found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> à Pierrot in Saint-Césaire in 1979, dated to about 36,300 years ago, confirmed an overlap with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cro-magnon">Cro-Magnons</a>. The Santoni made the oppidum at Pons their political and trading hub in the La Tène period, and after the Gallic War Mediolanum Santonum, now Saintes, became the first capital of the Roman province of Aquitaine under Augustus. It was burned by the Alamanni in 276. In 418 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> settled in the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> ousted them after defeating Alaric at Vouillé in 507.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> grew in the twelfth century under the Dukes of Aquitaine and received a communal charter from Henry II in 1175. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>'s marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry Plantagenet</a> in 1152 tied the region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Hundred Years' War devastated it until the French recaptured Montguyon in 1451. La Rochelle became a Protestant stronghold in the Wars of Religion. In 1627 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> began a siege of the city, with a dike blocking its access to the sea, and famine and disease reduced the population from 28,000 to 5,000 before it surrendered on 28 October 1628. Explorers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons">Pierre Dugua de Mons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, founded Québec in 1608.</p><p>The nineteenth century brought prosperity under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a>, driven by cognac, until the phylloxera crisis. German forces occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and built sea defences, including pillboxes on Oléron. Royan was nearly destroyed by an RAF raid on 5 January 1945, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> was freed on 9 May 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> lies in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine. The islands of Ré and Oléron belong to it. The Paléosite centre at Saint-Césaire, opened in 2005, presents the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthal</a> find, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a> keeps Roman monuments such as a votive arch and an amphitheatre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charente-Maritime” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Santones lived north of the Gironde estuary, along the Charente, in the modern <b>Charente-Maritime</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/santones">Santones</a></li></ul></section>
